linux_fb, i915: freeze the boot framebuffer as a Disable/Enable pair

vt_freeze_main_vd(), reached from register_fictitious_range(), sets
FB_FLAG_NOWRITE on the boot framebuffer (efifb) when a DRM driver's
aperture overlaps it. With the boot splash up that freezes the spinner from
i915's fbdev create, or from early in amdgpu init, until the console moves.

Make the freeze Darwin's console-op pair:

- vt_freeze_main_vd() sends kPEDisableScreen (with the frozen fb_info)
  before setting FB_FLAG_NOWRITE, so the spinner stops touching the old
  mapping. __register_framebuffer() already sends the matching
  kPEEnableScreen once the console has moved to the fbdev buffer (0009);
  the pair is sent at most once, so that Disable is not repeated. If the
  driver goes away first, vt_unfreeze_main_vd() closes the pair.
- i915 reusing the BIOS framebuffer calls the new
  register_fictitious_range_inherited(). The BIOS framebuffer stays pinned
  at its GGTT offset (PIN_OFFSET_FIXED), so when the fbdev base equals the
  base vt(4) draws on and the console is in graphics mode, there is no
  freeze at all and the spinner keeps turning through i915 attach.
- amdgpu and radeon keep the freeze (BAR resize can move efifb): the
  spinner pauses during init and resumes, with the splash still on screen,
  after the switch.

Text mode (-v, -s, serial, older kernels) sends no op and keeps today's
freeze, i915 included. kPEDisableScreen is invoked by name with its value
(7) repeated from nextbsd-kernel's <sys/pexpert.h>, as in 0009.

Refs nextbsd/nextbsd-kernel-extensions-graphics#112.

